sections.body:
Here's the version with one lock per account. A transfer locks the account it debits, then the account it credits, so the debit and the credit still happen as one step:

```java
// Naive: correct alone, freezes in pairs
void transfer(Account from, Account to, long cents) {
    synchronized (from) {
        synchronized (to) {
            from.debit(cents);
            to.credit(cents);
        }
    }
}
// thread 1: transfer(a, b, 100)        thread 2: transfer(b, a, 50)
```

```kotlin
// Naive: correct alone, freezes in pairs
fun transfer(from: Account, to: Account, cents: Long) {
    synchronized(from) {
        synchronized(to) {
            from.debit(cents)
            to.credit(cents)
        }
    }
}
// thread 1: transfer(a, b, 100)        thread 2: transfer(b, a, 50)
```

Each call is correct on its own, and transfers between different pairs of accounts now run side by side, which was the point. The trouble starts when two transfers touch the same two accounts in opposite directions. A marketplace's fee account and a busy merchant's account do that all day: fees flow one way, and refunds flow the other.

Let's trace the smallest case. Thread 1 runs `transfer(a, b, 100)` and locks `a`. At the same moment, thread 2 runs `transfer(b, a, 50)` and locks `b`. Thread 1 now asks for `b`, which thread 2 holds. Thread 2 asks for `a`, which thread 1 holds. Each releases its first lock only after it gets its second, so neither ever will. A third transfer that needs `a` arrives a second later and joins the line behind them, for good.

Those two threads will wait for each other forever: that's a **deadlock**. Nothing throws, and nothing logs. Both threads sit in `BLOCKED`, CPU drops to zero, and every request that needs either account piles up behind them.

The nasty part is timing. Both threads must take their first lock inside the same tiny window, so a test that runs one transfer at a time never sees it. Under load, with thousands of transfers a second between the same busy accounts, it's only a matter of when. The simulator below lets you play the scheduler and find the freeze by hand.

The Try it widens the window with a 300 ms sleep, so it freezes every time. Its last lines come from asking the JVM which threads are deadlocked, and the JVM answers with names. On a real pod, you'd ask from outside, before anyone restarts it. That's next.

code.src:
package workbench.concurrency.l06;

import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;
import java.lang.management.ThreadInfo;
import java.lang.management.ThreadMXBean;

/** Two transfers lock the same two accounts in opposite orders. Run with -Pargs=wait to keep it alive for jcmd. */
public class DeadlockDemo {
    static final Object accountA = new Object();
    static final Object accountB = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        boolean stayAlive = args.length > 0 && args[0].equals("wait");
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> move(accountA, accountB), "transfer-A-to-B");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> move(accountB, accountA), "transfer-B-to-A");
        t1.setDaemon(true);                  // daemon, so the JVM can still exit at the end
        t2.setDaemon(true);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        Thread.sleep(1_000);

        ThreadMXBean mx = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean();
        long[] ids = mx.findDeadlockedThreads();   // what a health check can ask the JVM
        System.out.println("deadlocked threads: " + (ids == null ? 0 : ids.length));
        if (ids != null) {
            for (ThreadInfo info : mx.getThreadInfo(ids)) {
                System.out.println("  " + info.getThreadName() + " is " + info.getThreadState()
                        + ", wants a lock held by " + info.getLockOwnerName());
            }
        }
        if (stayAlive) {
            System.out.println("pid " + ProcessHandle.current().pid() + ": run  jcmd "
                    + ProcessHandle.current().pid() + " Thread.print  in another terminal (60 s)");
            Thread.sleep(60_000);
        }
    }

    static void move(Object from, Object to) {
        synchronized (from) {
            sleep(300);                      // widen the window: the other thread takes its first lock now
            synchronized (to) {
                System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " moved the money");
            }
        }
    }

    static void sleep(long ms) {
        try { Thread.sleep(ms); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
    }
}

