package workbench.concurrency.l06

import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory

// Two transfers lock the same two accounts in opposite orders. Run with -Pargs=wait to keep it alive for jcmd.
private val accountA = Any()
private val accountB = Any()

fun main(args: Array<String>) {
    val stayAlive = args.firstOrNull() == "wait"
    val t1 = Thread({ move(accountA, accountB) }, "transfer-A-to-B").apply { isDaemon = true; start() }
    val t2 = Thread({ move(accountB, accountA) }, "transfer-B-to-A").apply { isDaemon = true; start() }
    Thread.sleep(1_000)

    val mx = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean()
    val ids = mx.findDeadlockedThreads()           // what a health check can ask the JVM
    println("deadlocked threads: ${ids?.size ?: 0}")
    ids?.let { mx.getThreadInfo(it) }?.forEach { info ->
        println("  ${info.threadName} is ${info.threadState}, wants a lock held by ${info.lockOwnerName}")
    }
    if (stayAlive) {
        val pid = ProcessHandle.current().pid()
        println("pid $pid: run  jcmd $pid Thread.print  in another terminal (60 s)")
        Thread.sleep(60_000)
    }
}

private fun move(from: Any, to: Any) {
    synchronized(from) {
        Thread.sleep(300)                          // widen the window: the other thread takes its first lock now
        synchronized(to) {
            println("${Thread.currentThread().name} moved the money")
        }
    }
}

sections.body:
Circular wait needs two threads that disagree about which lock comes first. So let's make them agree. Give every account a stable, unique key, and always lock the lower key first, whichever way the money moves:

```java
// Fixed: every transfer locks the lower id first
void transfer(Account from, Account to, long cents) {
    Account first = from.id() < to.id() ? from : to;   // one order for everyone
    Account second = first == from ? to : from;
    synchronized (first) {
        synchronized (second) {
            from.debit(cents);
            to.credit(cents);
        }
    }
}
```

```kotlin
// Fixed: every transfer locks the lower id first
fun transfer(from: Account, to: Account, cents: Long) {
    val first = if (from.id < to.id) from else to      // one order for everyone
    val second = if (first === from) to else from
    synchronized(first) {
        synchronized(second) {
            from.debit(cents)
            to.credit(cents)
        }
    }
}
```

Read the two lines that pick the locks as a sentence: the first lock is the account with the lower id, and the second is the other one. Now `transfer(a, b)` and `transfer(b, a)` both lock `a` first. Whoever gets `a` runs. The other waits for `a` while holding nothing, which is plain contention: it waits its turn, then goes.

The Try it below runs the exact traffic that froze the naive version: 200,000 transfers between the same two accounts, half in each direction. Correctness first: the total stayed at 2,000,000. Then time: it finished in 33 to 50 ms on runs here. With the locks put back in argument order, it hung on five runs out of six, anywhere from about 1,500 to 53,000 transfers in. The sixth run finished, which is the same bug, hiding.

Three details make or break the fix.

The key must be unique and must never change. An account id or an IBAN works. `hashCode()` doesn't: two different objects can share one, and then two threads may disagree about the order again.

The rule must be global. Each method locking in a sensible-looking order isn't enough. One method that locks the account and then the customer, and another that locks the customer and then the account, rebuild the cycle. Put the ordering in one helper and make every caller use it.

**A lock order only works if every caller follows it. Write it once, and use it everywhere.**

The order must also survive calls you don't see. A listener or a repository called while you hold a lock may take locks of its own, in its own order. That's the alien-code rule from synchronized and intrinsic locks, and the worked example below finds a deadlock hiding exactly there.

Database rows follow the same rule. Two transactions that lock the same two account rows with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, in opposite orders, deadlock in Oracle too, and locking rows in account-id order cures it.

A lock order works when you know every lock up front. Sometimes you don't, for example when the second account is known only after you've read the first. For that, we need a lock that can give up.

