package workbench.concurrency.l06

import kotlin.concurrent.thread

// The same opposite transfers, with every thread taking the locks in one global order.
private class Acct(val id: Int, var balance: Long)

fun main() {
    val a = Acct(1, 1_000_000)
    val b = Acct(2, 1_000_000)
    val t0 = System.nanoTime()
    val t1 = thread(name = "A-to-B") { repeat(100_000) { move(a, b, 1) } }
    val t2 = thread(name = "B-to-A") { repeat(100_000) { move(b, a, 1) } }
    t1.join()
    t2.join()
    println("200,000 opposite transfers in %d ms, total still %,d"
        .format((System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000, a.balance + b.balance))
}

private fun move(from: Acct, to: Acct, cents: Long) {
    val first = if (from.id < to.id) from else to    // lower id first, whatever the direction
    val second = if (first === from) to else from
    synchronized(first) {
        synchronized(second) {
            from.balance -= cents
            to.balance += cents
        }
    }
}

sections.body:
Every lock so far waits as long as it takes, which is the no-preemption condition at work. `ReentrantLock`, the lock in the lab's `Account`, offers a way out. Like `synchronized`, its `lock()` waits until the lock is free. Unlike it, `tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)` waits at most 10 ms and returns `false` if the lock is still taken. (The next lesson covers `ReentrantLock` in full.) So a transfer can try for both locks, and let go if it can't get them:

```java
if (from.lock().tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
    try {
        if (to.lock().tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try { /* move the money */ return true; }
            finally { to.lock().unlock(); }
        }
    } finally {
        from.lock().unlock();      // couldn't get both: let go of everything
    }
}
Thread.sleep(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(1, 10));   // random back-off, then retry
```

```kotlin
if (from.lock().tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
    try {
        if (to.lock().tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try { /* move the money */ return true }
            finally { to.lock().unlock() }
        }
    } finally {
        from.lock().unlock()       // couldn't get both: let go of everything
    }
}
Thread.sleep(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(1, 10))    // random back-off, then retry
```

First we try the account we debit, for 10 ms. If we get it, we try the other one, for 10 ms more. If both succeed, the money moves and we return. If the second fails, the `finally` gives back the first, so we hold nothing while we wait to retry. That release is the whole fix: a thread that can't finish lets go of everything.

The random back-off on the last line matters more than it looks. Leave it out, or sleep a fixed time, and two threads can fall into step: both take their first lock, both fail on the second, both release, and both retry at the same moment, over and over. That's **livelock**. The threads are busy and change state all the time, and nothing gets done. A thread dump looks healthy, which makes livelock harder to spot than deadlock.

The Try it shows a livelock with no locks at all. Two diners share one spoon, and each hands it over whenever the other is hungry. In one second, they passed it about 6 million times. Nobody ate. The fix is to break the symmetry: a random back-off, or a rule such as "after three polite turns, eat anyway". With that rule, they passed the spoon 6 times and both diners ate.

**Two threads that retry in step can retry forever. Back off for a random time.**

The third failure is **starvation**: the system makes progress, but one thread never does. A `ReentrantLock` is non-fair by default, so a thread that just released it can take it again before a waiter wakes up. That's faster overall, but one unlucky thread can wait a long time. `new ReentrantLock(true)` serves the longest waiter first instead, at some cost in throughput. A writer that never gets past a crowd of readers starves the same way, and lesson 12 of this track comes back to that.

A timeout, a lock that gives up and a fair queue are all things `synchronized` can't offer. They come from the explicit locks, and the second part of the lab puts `tryLock` to work.

code.src:
package workbench.concurrency.l06;

/** Two polite diners share one spoon. Each hands it over while the other is hungry. Try -Pargs=fixed. */
public class LivelockDemo {
    static volatile int spoonOwner = 0;                 // diner 0 or diner 1
    static final boolean[] hungry = {true, true};       // written by its owner only, read by the other
    static volatile boolean stop;
    static final long[] handovers = new long[2];

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        boolean fixed = args.length > 0 && args[0].equals("fixed");
        Thread d0 = new Thread(() -> dine(0, fixed), "diner-0");
        Thread d1 = new Thread(() -> dine(1, fixed), "diner-1");
        d0.start();
        d1.start();
        d0.join(1_000);
        d1.join(1_000);
        stop = true;
        d0.join();
        d1.join();
        System.out.printf("spoon handed over %,d times; meals eaten: %d%n",
                handovers[0] + handovers[1], (hungry[0] ? 0 : 1) + (hungry[1] ? 0 : 1));
    }

    static void dine(int me, boolean fixed) {
        int other = 1 - me;
        int politeTurns = 0;
        while (!stop) {
            if (spoonOwner != me) {                     // not my spoon: wait for it
                Thread.onSpinWait();
                continue;
            }
            boolean otherHungry;
            synchronized (hungry) { otherHungry = hungry[other]; }
            if (otherHungry && (!fixed || politeTurns < 3)) {
                politeTurns++;
                handovers[me]++;
                spoonOwner = other;                     // "you first": both keep saying it
                continue;
            }
            synchronized (hungry) { hungry[me] = false; } // eat
            spoonOwner = other;                         // then pass the spoon on
            return;
        }
    }
}

